      "chunk_id": "text_18",
      "quote": "The results of opioid efficacy for studies with data on a short-­ term follow-­ up (≤3 days) 38-­ 41 or without the study at high risk of bias 37 were similar as those reported from the main analysis (SMD 0.12, 95% CI -0.20 to 0.45, I 2 = 77%; and SMD 0.12, 95% CI -0.12 to 0.37, I 2 = 69%; Supplement Figures S2 and S3 ). Adult patients treated with opioids had better pain relief than those treated with nonopioids (SMD 0.28, 95% CI 0.13-­ 0.42, I 2 = 0%; Supplement Figure S4 ) contrary to children who had nonsignificant difference between opioids and nonopioids ( p = 0.78). However, the comparison of studies with pediatric versus adult patients showed no statistically significant difference between these subgroups ( χ 2 = 1.78, p = 0.18). When we removed the study 39 that had a placebo as a comparator in the adult subgroup, the effect was similar but was no longer statistically significant (SMD 0.16, 95% CI -0.06 to 0.38, I 2 = 0%; Supplement Figure S5 ). The comparison of studies using codeine versus opioids other than codeine showed a significant difference between these subgroups ( χ 2 = 7.47, p = 0.006). Codeine was not more effective than nonopioid medications (SMD -0.12, 95% CI -0.38 to 0.14, | I 2 = 19%). However, patients treated with other opioids than codeine had better pain relief than those treated with nonopioids (SMD 0.30, 95% CI 0.15-­ 0.45, I 2 = 0%; Figure 4 ). Results without the only study at high risk of bias 37 were identical for opioids other than codeine since that study was done with codeine (Supplement Figure S6 ). Also, when removing the study 39 that had a placebo as a comparator in the opioids other than codeine subgroup, the direction of the effect remained but was no longer statistically significant ( p = 0.06, SMD 0.22, 95% CI -0.01 to 0.44, I 2 = 0%; Supplement Figure S7 ).",
