      "quote": "Our review is limited by the study population's heterogeneity (pediatric and adult), medications type/dosage, and follow-­ up duration. However, analyses were done using a random-­ effect | model, which partially compensated for this diversity and after excluding codeine, the heterogeneity was very low ( I 2 = 0%). Furthermore, results were consistent in sensitivity analyses on the adult population and in which the study with high risk of bias and the one with only placebo as a comparison was excluded. Also, we studied only opioid use after ED discharge; results could be different in another context. Most studies utilized extensive exclusion criteria and included only patients with musculoskeletal injuries in a North American population, which limit their external validity. The adult studies included only painful syndromes of sprains and strains, and none had fractures that might have been better relieved by opioids. Most included studies (five of six) used a combination of acetaminophen (one also added an NSAID) and opioid (two of them at higher acetaminophen doses than what is currently permitted) instead of opioid alone, which may have contributed to opioid efficacy. Finally, included studies were not designed to assess the risk of overdose, opioid use disorder, or long-­ term harms.",
